/// Lesson sections for the info page — never shown on the practice screen.
const List<TechniqueSection> techGhostAccentContrastLesson = [
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Why",
    body:
        "Ghost notes are the notes you feel more than hear. Next to big accents they make a groove sound alive instead of mechanical; this contrast is the core of funk, R&B, hip-hop and every good shuffle. It is also the hardest dynamic job for the hands: the loudest and the quietest note, one sixteenth apart.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "How",
    body:
        "Ghost note: the stick starts barely 1 to 2 cm above the pad and just touches it, much softer than a normal tap. Accent: full height, about 25 cm. Moving from accent to ghost is a down stroke (stop low after the accent); moving from ghost to accent is an up stroke (the ghost lifts the stick). The grip stays loose; ghosts come from low height, not from squeezing.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Tips",
    body:
        "Ghosts should be barely audible, but audible: you (and the microphone) must still hear every one. If they sound like taps, go lower; if they vanish, lift a touch. The accents must not shrink because the ghosts are low. Line 4 is the funk backbeat on 2 and 4. Line 6 ghosts two notes with the same hand. Line 7 is a shuffle feel in triplets: accent, ghost, ghost.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Song examples",
    body:
        "Rosanna (Toto): Jeff Porcaro's half-time shuffle, full of ghost notes. Cissy Strut (The Meters): Zigaboo Modeliste's ghosted funk snare. Fool in the Rain (Led Zeppelin): ghost notes inside a half-time shuffle.",
  ),
];
